    "The FDA label provides quantitative data from a U.S. clinical trial of metformin hydrochloride tablets (up to 2,550 mg/day) in 141 patients with type 2 diabetes. Adverse events that occurred in more than 5 % of participants and were more common than placebo (n = 145) are listed with their incidence rates: diarrhea (53 % vs 12 % placebo), nausea/vomiting (26 % vs 8 %), flatulence (12 % vs 6 %), asthenia (9 % vs 6 %), indigestion (7 % vs 4 %), abdominal discomfort (6 % vs 5 %), and headache (6 % vs 5 %). Diarrhea led to study‑drug discontinuation in 6 % of metformin‑treated patients. In the same trial, a decrease to sub‑normal serum vitamin B12 levels was observed in about 7 % of patients after 29 weeks of therapy. These figures are the only numerical adverse‑reaction rates reported on the label【openFDA label:FDA-LABEL-metformin-2】.",
    "The label also mentions post‑marketing experience, noting that cholestatic, hepatocellular, and mixed hepatocellular liver injury have been reported with metformin use. However, because post‑marketing reports are voluntary and the size of the exposed population is unknown, the label explicitly states that it is not possible to reliably estimate the frequency of these liver‑injury events or to establish a causal relationship. Therefore, the label cannot provide a numerical incidence for liver injury based on post‑marketing data【openFDA label:FDA-LABEL-metformin-2】."
  ],
